WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:11.720
You know, usually when we talk about, like, a medical diagnosis or just understanding a basic biological function, there's this deeply ingrained expectation of absolute precision.

00:11.940 --> 00:12.360
Right.

00:12.460 --> 00:13.840
We want it to be like engineering.

00:14.380 --> 00:14.860
Exactly.

00:15.380 --> 00:16.800
You want to feel like it's mechanical.

00:16.800 --> 00:23.180
You fall off your bike, you break your arm, and the x-ray shows that jagged white line right across the bone.

00:23.240 --> 00:26.180
And the doctor just points at the film and says, well, there it is.

00:26.480 --> 00:29.980
Yeah, broken or not broken, it's totally clean.

00:30.180 --> 00:34.100
It makes complete sense to us because we can literally see the mechanism at work.

00:34.460 --> 00:36.180
And it is incredibly comforting, right?

00:36.300 --> 00:39.500
I mean, as a species, we really like things to be visible.

00:39.740 --> 00:40.260
Oh, absolutely.

00:40.460 --> 00:43.060
We want our biology to be neatly categorized.

00:43.060 --> 00:50.560
We want it to operate like a machine that we can just take apart, find the broken gear, and, you know, reverse engineer a fix.

00:50.660 --> 00:52.840
But then you step into the world of cognitive science.

00:52.920 --> 00:54.400
Yeah, and that all goes out the window.

00:54.640 --> 00:55.500
It really does.

00:55.500 --> 01:03.920
You look at neurodevelopment, or you look at how the brain actually functions in the real world, and suddenly that x-ray machine is completely useless.

01:04.100 --> 01:04.920
Well, hardly useless.

01:05.300 --> 01:10.360
We are looking at a diagnostic landscape that is, well, it's murky at best.

01:10.600 --> 01:10.840
Yeah.

01:10.840 --> 01:13.080
And here is the truly wild part.

01:13.220 --> 01:13.720
Go for it.

01:13.820 --> 01:24.700
What if the entire foundation of modern neuroscience, and artificial intelligence for that matter, is based on a fundamental hallucination by the scientists themselves?

01:24.700 --> 01:27.900
I mean, that is the exact premise we are looking at today.

01:28.000 --> 01:28.220
Yeah.

01:28.440 --> 01:37.600
Because for the last decade, cognitive science, neuroscience, AI, they've all been entirely captivated by one central dogma.

01:37.660 --> 01:38.680
The prediction machine.

01:38.720 --> 01:39.060
Exactly.

01:39.200 --> 01:42.460
The idea that the brain is, at its core, a prediction machine.

01:42.840 --> 01:52.500
A biological device that constantly generates statistical guesses about the external world, and updates its own software by minimizing the errors in those guesses.

01:52.500 --> 01:55.020
Right. The predictive processing framework. It's everywhere.

01:55.220 --> 01:56.440
Oh, you can't escape it.

01:56.500 --> 02:00.960
Seriously. You can't read a single paper on AI or human psychology right now without tripping over it.

02:01.040 --> 02:08.340
It has become the absolute dominant paradigm, but it might also be the absolute definition of diagnostic muddy waters.

02:08.360 --> 02:08.800
This is hard.

02:09.040 --> 02:18.500
Well, primarily because we have slowly, collectively, started treating a mathematical metaphor as if it were a literal, physical, biological reality.

02:18.500 --> 02:27.380
Okay. Let's unpack this. Because today, for this deep dive, we are exploring a dense, totally paradigm-shifting paper from 2026.

02:27.500 --> 02:30.040
Yes. By an independent researcher named Flyxion.

02:30.320 --> 02:36.560
Right. It's titled, Beyond Prediction Error Toward an Ecological and Topological Theory of Cognition.

02:37.220 --> 02:39.760
And the core argument is just, I mean, it's explosive.

02:40.020 --> 02:40.740
It really is.

02:40.740 --> 02:47.940
Flyxion is suggesting that our entire understanding of the brain as a prediction machine is fundamentally, catastrophically backwards.

02:48.220 --> 02:53.500
Yeah. That we are essentially mistaking the scientists' measuring tools for the organism's actual machinery.

02:53.760 --> 02:57.200
So setting the stage here, what is the core mission of our deep dive today?

02:57.560 --> 03:03.120
The core mission is to systematically dismantle what Flyxion calls the computational metaphor of the brain.

03:03.380 --> 03:03.700
Okay.

03:03.700 --> 03:08.780
We are going to strip away the assumptions that have completely bogged down neuroscience and AI for years.

03:09.320 --> 03:13.640
And then we are going to rebuild our understanding of the mind from the ground up.

03:13.720 --> 03:15.060
Using, what was it, geometry?

03:15.500 --> 03:18.320
Geometry, ecology, and topology.

03:19.080 --> 03:22.080
We want to figure out what it actually means to have agency.

03:22.640 --> 03:32.060
What it actually means to be a living, persisting system, rather than just, you know, a biological calculator trying to minimize a mathematical loss function.

03:32.060 --> 03:40.120
And we are going to translate these incredibly high-level physics and math concepts into some massive aha moments.

03:40.260 --> 03:41.020
Feelings for me to try.

03:41.220 --> 03:41.780
We will.

03:42.220 --> 03:52.800
I promise you, the listener, this will completely change how you view your own mind, your memories, the way you form habits, and even how you understand mental health.

03:52.900 --> 03:54.020
So welcome to the conversation.

03:54.320 --> 03:54.940
Yeah, welcome.

03:55.160 --> 04:01.020
To really grasp how radical this paper is, we have to start by looking at how we got trapped in this mindset in the first place.

04:01.220 --> 04:01.660
Definitely.

04:02.060 --> 04:07.200
Because the history of cognitive science is basically a history of getting stuck in our own metaphors.

04:07.760 --> 04:08.240
It really is.

04:08.300 --> 04:12.180
It is a fascinating, almost comical history of borrowed technology.

04:12.720 --> 04:15.160
If you trace it back, you see this undeniable pattern.

04:15.920 --> 04:29.140
Whenever humanity invents a major civilization-altering piece of technology, we immediately, almost reflectively, assume the human brain must work exactly like that new technology.

04:29.140 --> 04:32.420
We just project our newest convention right onto our own biology.

04:32.500 --> 04:32.900
Exactly.

04:33.040 --> 04:40.200
I remember reading about this in the 19th century when the telegraph was the absolute cutting edge of human achievement.

04:40.320 --> 04:40.740
Oh, yeah.

04:40.820 --> 04:41.660
The telegraph metaphor.

04:41.820 --> 04:41.960
Right.

04:42.360 --> 04:46.400
Scientists started describing the nervous system as a vast communications network.

04:46.620 --> 04:46.880
Yeah.

04:46.880 --> 04:53.320
They literally used the language of wires, switchboards, and electrical pulses being routed by operators.

04:53.320 --> 04:54.120
Precisely.

04:54.340 --> 04:54.500
Yeah.

04:54.540 --> 04:59.420
And then you move into the mid-20th century, and we developed the programmable digital computer.

04:59.520 --> 05:00.160
A huge leap.

05:00.400 --> 05:00.860
Massive.

05:00.960 --> 05:03.960
It changed the world, and overnight, the biological metaphors shifted.

05:04.520 --> 05:06.920
Suddenly, the brain wasn't a switchboard anymore.

05:07.040 --> 05:07.640
It was a computer.

05:07.820 --> 05:09.560
It was a symbol manipulation device.

05:10.340 --> 05:14.340
Minds were described in formal academic literature as Turing machines.

05:14.400 --> 05:14.840
Oh, wow.

05:14.840 --> 05:17.560
Human perception was relabeled as input.

05:18.040 --> 05:20.140
Human memory was relabeled as storage.

05:20.740 --> 05:22.060
Reasoning was processing.

05:22.740 --> 05:25.940
We wholesale adopted the entire vocabulary of computer science.

05:26.060 --> 05:28.940
Hardware, software, encoding, retrieving, all of it.

05:29.120 --> 05:29.620
All of it.

05:29.760 --> 05:33.520
And because it sort of made intuitive sense, we just ran with it.

05:33.620 --> 05:36.000
But now we live in the era of machine learning.

05:36.260 --> 05:36.660
Exactly.

05:36.780 --> 05:42.340
We live in the era of Bayesian statistics and predictive algorithms running on supercomputers.

05:42.340 --> 05:47.280
So, unsurprisingly, the new metaphor is that the brain is a prediction error minimizer.

05:48.040 --> 05:50.700
The brain is running Bayesian inference algorithms.

05:51.100 --> 05:53.160
Which brings us to Flyxion's point.

05:53.860 --> 05:57.160
They point out a massive, glaring issue with this entire progression.

05:57.280 --> 05:59.180
They call it the reification error.

05:59.380 --> 06:00.280
The reification error.

06:00.320 --> 06:00.460
Yeah.

06:00.600 --> 06:02.340
Definition 1.1 in the text, right?

06:02.420 --> 06:02.680
Yes.

06:03.000 --> 06:04.540
This is the crucial starting point.

06:04.780 --> 06:10.400
To truly understand this error, we have to look very closely at what scientists actually do when they study a living organism.

06:10.580 --> 06:11.340
Okay, walk me through.

06:11.340 --> 06:14.480
Let's say you are a researcher observing a complex living creature.

06:14.940 --> 06:17.720
Let's say a fox navigating a forest environment.

06:17.960 --> 06:18.980
A fox in the woods.

06:19.100 --> 06:19.480
Got it.

06:19.760 --> 06:23.840
That fox has an infinitely complex high-dimensional reality.

06:24.720 --> 06:30.480
It has a cellular metabolism, a unique developmental history, a social environment.

06:30.880 --> 06:35.380
Physical forces acting on its muscles, chemical gradients in its bloodstream.

06:35.760 --> 06:36.360
All of that.

06:36.360 --> 06:45.920
In the paper, Flyxion mathematically denotes this full, staggering reality as the variable X, the complete ecological trajectory space.

06:45.980 --> 06:46.080
Right.

06:46.140 --> 06:51.240
But a scientist with a clipboard and some cameras cannot possibly measure all of X.

06:51.320 --> 06:52.280
No, it's way too big.

06:52.280 --> 06:54.080
It's totally computationally impossible.

06:54.280 --> 06:57.120
You can't track every molecule and every memory simultaneously.

06:57.260 --> 06:57.740
Exactly.

06:57.740 --> 06:59.440
So what does the scientist do?

06:59.700 --> 07:07.600
They take their measuring tools, their statistical software, and their prior assumptions, and they create a compressed mathematical model of the fox.

07:07.740 --> 07:08.860
A simplified version.

07:08.920 --> 07:10.760
A vastly simplified representation.

07:11.100 --> 07:12.960
Let's call that compressed model M.

07:12.960 --> 07:16.460
Okay, so we have X, the real fox, and M, the math model.

07:16.600 --> 07:16.840
Right.

07:17.040 --> 07:22.320
The scientist looks at M, uses it to run simulations, and uses it to predict what the fox will do next.

07:22.760 --> 07:25.300
And usually because the math is good, it works pretty well.

07:25.520 --> 07:26.320
Which is fine, right?

07:26.400 --> 07:31.480
I mean, science is entirely about making useful models that simplify reality so we can understand it.

07:31.620 --> 07:33.500
Making the model is perfectly fine.

07:33.960 --> 07:37.040
The model M is a highly effective scientific tool.

07:37.440 --> 07:40.160
It has massive predictive validity for the observer.

07:40.820 --> 07:41.740
So where does it go wrong?

07:41.740 --> 07:45.040
The reification error happens in the very next step.

07:45.720 --> 07:53.120
The error is when the scientist looks at how brilliantly their model M works on their computer screen and concludes, uh-huh.

07:53.620 --> 07:54.900
Oh, I see where this is going.

07:55.260 --> 07:59.980
They say because this mathematical model predicts the organism's behavior so well,

08:00.360 --> 08:05.760
the organism itself must literally have this exact model M inside its own head.

08:05.980 --> 08:08.980
Computing those exact same equations to decide what to do.

08:09.220 --> 08:09.740
Exactly.

08:09.740 --> 08:12.400
It is confusing the map for the territory.

08:12.580 --> 08:13.700
In fact, it's worse than that.

08:13.820 --> 08:17.180
It is letting the map completely consume and erase the territory.

08:17.480 --> 08:17.860
Okay, wait.

08:18.480 --> 08:22.560
I want to push back on this a little because I want to make sure I'm not misunderstanding something fundamental here.

08:22.680 --> 08:22.940
Sure.

08:23.080 --> 08:23.480
Push back.

08:23.480 --> 08:28.440
Because if I read a modern neuroscience paper, they talk about neural coding all the time.

08:28.640 --> 08:28.860
Mm-hmm.

08:28.860 --> 08:31.200
And they seem to have the physical evidence to back it up.

08:31.340 --> 08:31.600
Right.

08:31.680 --> 08:32.380
The grid cells.

08:32.540 --> 08:32.720
Yeah.

08:33.220 --> 08:44.780
We can literally put electrodes in a rat's brain, put the rat in a maze, and watch specific grid cells fire precisely when the rat crosses a specific spatial coordinate.

08:44.780 --> 08:46.480
It is highly reliable, yes.

08:46.600 --> 08:47.780
That's a spatial map.

08:47.900 --> 08:48.760
That's not a metaphor.

08:49.000 --> 08:52.440
That is a literal physical mapping of the environment inside the brain.

08:53.220 --> 08:54.820
How can Flyxion dismiss that?

08:55.000 --> 09:01.520
That is exactly the pushback that the predictive processing community gives, and it is so vital to untangle it.

09:01.520 --> 09:02.900
Let's look at your rat example.

09:03.020 --> 09:03.260
Okay.

09:03.860 --> 09:11.820
The firing of the cell, the electrical action potential, the release of neurotransmitters that is an objective physical biological reality.

09:12.040 --> 09:12.240
Right.

09:12.240 --> 09:18.700
The fact that the cell consistently fires when the rat is in the northeast corner of the maze is also an objective physical reality.

09:19.140 --> 09:21.800
But calling that relationship a map or a code?

09:21.960 --> 09:22.180
Yeah.

09:22.380 --> 09:23.300
That is the metaphor.

09:23.480 --> 09:25.100
Wait, isn't that just arguing over semantics?

09:25.280 --> 09:28.020
If it fires reliably in the corner, it's encoding the corner.

09:28.220 --> 09:29.160
It is not semantics.

09:29.360 --> 09:30.660
It is the entire problem.

09:30.660 --> 09:32.580
Ask yourself, to whom is it a map?

09:32.740 --> 09:33.540
To the rat.

09:33.780 --> 09:35.760
No, to the scientist watching from above.

09:35.920 --> 09:36.400
Oh, wow.

09:36.740 --> 09:41.580
The information processing interpretation is layered on top of the physical reality by the observer.

09:42.360 --> 09:44.980
The scientist already has a bird's eye view of the maze.

09:45.420 --> 09:48.180
The scientist already knows where the northeast corner is.

09:48.500 --> 09:51.460
The scientist sees the neuron fire in that corner.

09:51.740 --> 09:56.300
And the scientist declares, oh, this neuron is processing spatial information about the corner.

09:56.460 --> 09:57.300
Oh, I see.

09:57.800 --> 09:59.260
The rat doesn't have the bird's eye view.

09:59.300 --> 09:59.740
Exactly.

09:59.740 --> 10:03.920
The rat is just existing in a continuous flow of sensorimotor coupling.

10:04.640 --> 10:09.640
The rat's cells are changing their state to maintain the rat's viability in its environment.

10:10.220 --> 10:11.480
So it's just reacting physically?

10:11.700 --> 10:12.020
Yes.

10:12.680 --> 10:19.000
The concept of information processing imports the scientist's external, omniscient knowledge into the rat's brain

10:19.000 --> 10:25.000
and treats that knowledge as if it were a literal mechanical substance moving through the synapses.

10:25.000 --> 10:26.400
Okay, here's an analogy.

10:27.120 --> 10:29.660
Tell me if this actually captures the reification error.

10:29.880 --> 10:30.380
Let's hear it.

10:30.380 --> 10:35.680
Let's say I'm an aerospace engineer and I am tracking a high-performance jet Flyxion through a storm.

10:35.760 --> 10:37.300
Okay, a jet in a storm.

10:37.300 --> 10:45.380
I build a massive, incredibly complex mathematical physics model on my supercomputer to predict the jet's exact path.

10:45.380 --> 10:52.380
I'm calculating air density, fluid dynamics, the thermodynamics of the thrust, the exact physics of the curvature.

10:52.380 --> 10:53.720
Very complex math.

10:53.720 --> 10:58.360
Right, and my model is flawlessly accurate at predicting where the jet will be in 10 seconds.

10:58.600 --> 11:07.780
But if I take a blue torch, cut open the jet's engine, and look inside, well, I'm not going to find my fluid dynamic equations floating around in there.

11:07.800 --> 11:08.060
Dang it.

11:08.060 --> 11:09.740
The engine isn't calculating thermodynamics.

11:10.020 --> 11:12.680
The engine is just combusting jet fuel and turning turbines.

11:13.800 --> 11:18.440
Are neuroscientists essentially cutting open the brain and looking for their own fluid dynamic equations?

11:18.660 --> 11:20.340
That is a phenomenal analogy.

11:20.840 --> 11:22.000
Yes, that is exactly it.

11:22.120 --> 11:23.420
The brain is the engine.

11:23.520 --> 11:33.120
It's combusting metabolic fuel, maintaining its internal temperatures, circulating ions, engaging in wildly complex physical and chemical processes to keep the organism alive.

11:33.140 --> 11:34.440
Because doing physical engine stuff.

11:34.500 --> 11:35.220
Yeah, right.

11:35.220 --> 11:41.880
And the scientist has built a beautiful, elegant, mathematical model of those processes from the outside.

11:42.280 --> 11:47.040
But then, looking at the biological engine, the scientist says, look, I found a circuit.

11:47.440 --> 11:50.840
This circuit must be calculating the fluid dynamics of the environment.

11:51.120 --> 11:52.700
That's crazy when you put it like that.

11:52.780 --> 11:53.280
It is.

11:53.620 --> 11:58.380
This fundamental confusion leads to what Flyxion calls epistemic phlogiston.

11:58.920 --> 12:00.000
Epistemic phlogiston.

12:00.000 --> 12:06.200
Let's break that down for the listener, because phlogiston is one of my absolute favorite weird historical science facts.

12:06.340 --> 12:07.440
It's a great piece of history.

12:07.600 --> 12:15.920
Phlogiston was this totally fake substance that 17th century chemists basically hallucinated to explain how fire worked, right?

12:16.000 --> 12:16.600
Precisely.

12:17.000 --> 12:25.060
Before humanity understood oxygen and chemical oxidation, scientists observed things burning and they observed metal rusting and they needed an explanation.

12:25.220 --> 12:26.100
They needed a mechanism.

12:26.100 --> 12:31.400
So they hypothesized a completely invisible, weightless substance called phlogiston.

12:31.860 --> 12:34.120
They said that wood was full of phlogiston.

12:34.340 --> 12:41.460
And when it burned, it released the phlogiston into the air until the air was full, which is why a fire goes out in a closed jar.

12:41.760 --> 12:44.080
I mean, it sounded highly scientific at the time.

12:44.420 --> 12:47.640
It unified all these different phenomena under one theory.

12:47.640 --> 12:49.200
But it was entirely circular.

12:49.620 --> 12:54.800
It explained absolute nothing because it was just a placeholder word for a chemical process.

12:55.380 --> 12:57.080
They didn't have the tools to understand yet.

12:57.200 --> 13:03.060
It was epistemic meaning related to their own knowledge, but they treated it as a physical substance.

13:03.480 --> 13:09.520
And Flyxion is arguing that information processing is the phlogiston of modern neuroscience.

13:09.520 --> 13:10.120
Yes.

13:10.840 --> 13:18.560
When a scientist says a brain region is processing spatial information, it sounds like a rigorous mechanical explanation of biology.

13:19.240 --> 13:25.740
But it is actually just describing what the scientist already knows about the environment projected onto the brain's activity.

13:25.840 --> 13:26.500
It was an illusion.

13:26.820 --> 13:29.580
It creates fundamentally ill-posed research questions.

13:30.380 --> 13:38.000
Scientists spend millions of dollars in grant money looking for the neural correlate of a computational operation that they themselves invented.

13:38.000 --> 13:40.860
They are looking for phlogiston in the synapses.

13:40.940 --> 13:53.080
Okay, so if information processing is a flawed metaphor, and if we are constantly committing this reification error, how on earth did predictive coding become the absolute theory of everything?

13:53.320 --> 13:55.000
That is the million-dollar question.

13:55.280 --> 13:56.300
I mean, we see it everywhere.

13:56.440 --> 13:57.180
It's in psychology.

13:57.460 --> 13:58.120
It's in sociology.

13:58.120 --> 14:00.140
It's the entire basis of modern AI.

14:00.820 --> 14:02.660
Why did we all buy into it so hard?

14:02.660 --> 14:09.660
To figure that out, we have to look at how the actual word prediction got completely hijacked from its original meaning.

14:09.760 --> 14:10.200
Hijacked.

14:10.600 --> 14:14.160
We do, because it didn't start as a theory of human consciousness.

14:14.500 --> 14:18.540
We have to go back to the original, highly technical definition of predictive coding.

14:19.080 --> 14:25.080
It originated in telecommunications and signal processing long before any neuroscientist ever touched it.

14:25.460 --> 14:28.580
The paper specifically cites Rao and Ballard from 1999.

14:28.840 --> 14:29.100
Right.

14:29.100 --> 14:35.820
They brought it into visual neuroscience, but originally this was literally just a math trick to save bandwidth on a copper wire, wasn't it?

14:36.100 --> 14:36.600
Exactly.

14:37.100 --> 14:39.320
Let's think about early digital video transmission.

14:40.020 --> 14:44.420
Imagine you are transmitting a video signal over a network with very limited bandwidth.

14:44.620 --> 14:45.900
Like an old dial-up connection.

14:46.180 --> 14:46.500
Right.

14:46.700 --> 14:52.280
Most of the time, the next frame of a video is almost completely identical to the previous frame.

14:52.760 --> 14:56.240
If a news anchor is sitting at a desk, the background doesn't change.

14:56.400 --> 14:57.540
Only their lips move.

14:57.540 --> 15:00.920
Oh, sure. The desk is the same. The wall is the same.

15:01.160 --> 15:08.580
If you transmit every single pixel of every single frame, 30 frames a second, you are sending a massive amount of redundant data.

15:08.960 --> 15:10.420
You will crash the network.

15:10.560 --> 15:11.460
Which is highly inefficient.

15:11.640 --> 15:12.360
Incredibly inefficient.

15:12.980 --> 15:16.680
So signal processing engineers came up with a brilliant mathematical trick.

15:16.940 --> 15:17.480
Yeah, they do.

15:17.480 --> 15:26.620
Instead of sending the whole image, the computer on the receiving end generates a local prediction of what the next frame will look like, based entirely on the past frames.

15:27.040 --> 15:30.200
The computer on the sending end does the exact same thing.

15:30.760 --> 15:36.220
Then, the sending end only transmits the difference between the actual next frame and the predicted frame.

15:36.580 --> 15:38.320
That difference is the prediction error.

15:38.320 --> 15:45.920
Oh, wow. So if the news anchor doesn't move at all, the prediction is perfect, the error is zero, and you transmit absolutely nothing over the wire.

15:46.240 --> 15:49.040
You only send the pixels that surprise the system.

15:49.680 --> 15:53.440
That is brilliant. It mathematically minimizes the required bandwidth.

15:53.440 --> 16:03.120
It's a gorgeous, elegant engineering trick, and what Rao and Ballard did in 1999 was propose that maybe the mammalian visual cortex does this.

16:03.200 --> 16:04.300
Just as a way to save energy.

16:04.480 --> 16:11.520
Right. Maybe higher brain areas predict what lower brain areas are going to see, and the optic nerve only sends up the prediction errors.

16:11.960 --> 16:18.800
It was a way to explain how the brain processes immense amounts of visual data without running out of metabolic energy.

16:18.800 --> 16:23.420
And in that restricted technical sense, it's a really fascinating hypothesis.

16:24.360 --> 16:27.860
I mean, a prediction here has a very strict, narrow definition.

16:28.020 --> 16:32.560
It is a literal numerical estimate of a concurrent or future signal value.

16:32.880 --> 16:35.140
It is just math happening on a grid.

16:35.280 --> 16:36.700
But that is not where it stopped.

16:36.960 --> 16:37.440
Not at all.

16:37.660 --> 16:43.520
Over the last two decades, this precise technical engineering term underwent massive concept creep.

16:43.880 --> 16:46.360
It expanded to swallow absolutely everything.

16:46.600 --> 16:47.980
It became the theory of everything.

16:47.980 --> 16:52.300
Suddenly, organisms aren't just compressing visual signals on their optic nerve.

16:52.520 --> 16:54.540
They are predicting their sensory inputs.

16:54.840 --> 16:57.340
They are predicting the outcomes of their motor actions.

16:57.960 --> 17:02.360
They are maintaining prior predictions about the sociological structure of reality.

17:02.720 --> 17:12.280
The entire brain was completely reframed as a machine whose single defining paleological goal is to minimize prediction error.

17:12.280 --> 17:24.240
And Flyxion relies heavily on a brilliant critique by a researcher named Romain Brett to explain why this massive expansion of the theory is basically an intellectual sleight of hand.

17:24.340 --> 17:25.140
Okay, I want to hear this.

17:25.540 --> 17:31.100
Brett argues that the predictive processing framework constantly equivocates between prediction and anticipation.

17:31.560 --> 17:33.240
Between prediction and anticipation.

17:33.240 --> 17:38.400
What's fascinating here is the sheer difference between those two concepts when you actually define them.

17:38.900 --> 17:41.920
We use them interchangeably in casual, everyday conversation.

17:42.020 --> 17:42.440
All the time.

17:42.840 --> 17:45.720
Like, I predict it will rain versus I anticipate it will rain.

17:45.920 --> 17:49.300
But biologically and mathematically, they are completely different operations.

17:49.780 --> 17:50.640
Completely different.

17:50.640 --> 18:09.340
Brett's point is that the predictive processing theory uses the strict, rigorous mathematical machinery of prediction to sound highly scientific, but it constantly smuggles in the ordinary, everyday meaning of anticipation to make the theory seem like it can explain all of complex human behavior.

18:09.340 --> 18:16.340
Let's really ground this for the listener, because the source text provides three distinct examples of anticipation versus prediction.

18:16.540 --> 18:19.520
And when you see the difference, you really can't unsee it.

18:19.520 --> 18:21.580
It breaks the illusion completely.

18:21.760 --> 18:22.260
Yeah, it really does.

18:22.340 --> 18:25.020
Example number one, the cyanobacterium.

18:25.300 --> 18:28.680
This is a beautiful example of what we call anticipatory entrainment.

18:29.440 --> 18:32.440
Cyanobacteria are ancient single-celled organisms.

18:32.920 --> 18:34.160
They perform photosynthesis.

18:34.480 --> 18:34.640
Right.

18:34.640 --> 18:45.520
What microbiologists have observed is that before dawn breaks, hours before the sun actually rises and light hits the water, these bacteria begin physically decompacting their chromosomes.

18:45.700 --> 18:45.980
Oh, yeah.

18:45.980 --> 18:54.240
They are preparing their internal biochemical machinery for massive photosynthetic activity so that they are completely ready the exact moment the light hits.

18:54.240 --> 19:05.960
Now, if you are a predictive coding purist, you look at that single-celled organism, you say, aha, the bacterium has an internal generative statistical model of the solar system.

19:05.960 --> 19:06.980
Right, which is absurd.

19:06.980 --> 19:17.520
It is calculating the Bayesian probability of the Earth's rotation, generating a prior prediction of the sun rising, and acting to minimize the free energy of its prediction error.

19:17.840 --> 19:20.080
Which is, as you said, patently absurd.

19:20.660 --> 19:24.660
The cyanobacterium does not have an internal statistical model of the sun.

19:25.020 --> 19:30.560
It is not generating a numerical estimate of a future sensory signal on a biological hard drive.

19:30.560 --> 19:31.480
So, what is it doing?

19:31.960 --> 19:37.220
What is actually happening is that the organism is dynamically entrained to its environment.

19:37.740 --> 19:47.340
Its physical biochemical rhythms are literally physically coupled to the environmental periodicity of light and dark, built up over billions of years of evolution.

19:47.700 --> 19:51.800
It is anticipating the sun, but it is an anticipation completely without representation.

19:52.300 --> 19:52.680
Exactly.

19:52.820 --> 19:57.900
It's like if I have two tuning forks of the same pitch on a table and I strike one, the other one starts vibrating.

19:58.120 --> 19:58.860
Perfect analogy.

19:58.860 --> 20:01.580
The second tuning fork isn't predicting the sound wave.

20:01.940 --> 20:06.140
It's not running a Bayesian inference model of acoustics in its metal prongs.

20:06.380 --> 20:09.260
It's just physically coupled to the physics of the environment.

20:09.500 --> 20:09.940
Exactly.

20:10.440 --> 20:13.580
It is a physical resonance, not a mathematical computation.

20:14.180 --> 20:17.540
Now, let's scale it up to a human with a second example, the runner in the blocks.

20:17.820 --> 20:18.100
Okay.

20:18.400 --> 20:21.520
Imagine an Olympic sprinter waiting for the starting pistol.

20:21.980 --> 20:23.120
They are in the blocks.

20:23.560 --> 20:25.280
Their muscles are immensely tense.

20:25.500 --> 20:27.480
Their weight is shifted perfectly forward.

20:27.480 --> 20:30.240
Their attention is absolute and totally narrowed.

20:30.520 --> 20:32.680
They are deeply anticipating the gun.

20:33.160 --> 20:45.020
But they aren't sitting there in their head calculating, oh, I estimate the decibel level of the gunshot will be exactly 110 decibels arriving at my auditory cortex in precisely 1.4 seconds.

20:45.020 --> 20:48.120
No. And if they were doing that, they would lose the race.

20:48.120 --> 20:52.980
The tense bodily state is not a numerical estimate of an incoming sensory input.

20:53.440 --> 20:59.280
It is a practical action relative reorganization of the organism's entire physical state.

20:59.380 --> 21:00.440
They are just getting ready.

21:00.440 --> 21:01.160
Exactly.

21:01.720 --> 21:06.520
The runner's body is structurally configured to exploit an expected environmental event.

21:07.020 --> 21:13.320
It's about total readiness for action, not epistemic accuracy about the exact waveform of a sound.

21:13.560 --> 21:20.140
And the third example is something the listener probably did this week, grabbing an umbrella, because you look outside and see dark clouds.

21:20.140 --> 21:24.860
Right. When you grab an umbrella, you are undeniably anticipating rain.

21:25.240 --> 21:29.940
But you aren't doing it just to minimize the prediction error of feeling wet skin.

21:30.720 --> 21:32.440
How would the predictive model frame that?

21:32.720 --> 21:36.360
The predictive processing model would say you predict your skin will be dry.

21:36.700 --> 21:38.380
The rain causes a prediction error.

21:38.820 --> 21:43.080
You grab the umbrella to make the sensory input match your prediction of dryness.

21:43.180 --> 21:44.140
That seems so convoluted.

21:44.860 --> 21:46.880
Because it completely misses the biology.

21:46.880 --> 21:52.400
You grab the umbrella to reorganize your relationship with the environment to maintain your viability.

21:52.700 --> 21:53.980
You want to stay warm.

21:54.120 --> 21:55.220
You want to not get sick.

21:55.300 --> 21:56.640
You want to keep your clothes intact.

21:56.860 --> 21:56.960
Right.

21:57.160 --> 22:03.960
Your action is relative to your physical constraints as a biological organism, not just your sensory expectations.

22:04.620 --> 22:12.780
So to put it incredibly simply, technical prediction is about guessing exactly what sensory data is going to hit your retinas or your eardrums, pixel by pixel.

22:12.780 --> 22:20.880
Ecological anticipation is about dynamically changing your body and your behavior to stay alive and effective in a chaotic, changing world.

22:21.020 --> 22:21.240
Yes.

22:21.520 --> 22:28.060
And if we connect this to the bigger picture of cognitive science, it fundamentally changes how we view perception itself.

22:28.480 --> 22:31.780
Are you familiar with James J. Gibson's theory of affordances from the 1970s?

22:32.480 --> 22:33.300
I know the term.

22:33.480 --> 22:35.660
It's the idea that we don't just see shapes and colors.

22:35.720 --> 22:37.780
We see what objects allow us to do, right?

22:37.880 --> 22:38.320
Precisely.

22:38.660 --> 22:41.800
It was a massive paradigm shift in ecological psychology.

22:41.800 --> 23:02.740
If you look at a wooden chair, the computational predictive processing model says your retina takes in photons, sends raw data to the visual cortex, the brain runs a statistical inference over a generative model, matches the data to a stored symbolic representation of chair, and then a higher cognitive process deduces that logically you can sit on it.

23:02.960 --> 23:05.220
That's a lot of steps to just sit down.

23:05.560 --> 23:06.360
Gibson argued.

23:06.780 --> 23:10.140
And Flyxion completely agrees that this is entirely backwards.

23:10.140 --> 23:13.840
You perceive the chair directly as sit-able.

23:14.680 --> 23:23.280
The affordance, the possibility for action, is given in the perception itself, deeply mediated by the fact that you have a specific body with legs that bend at the knee.

23:23.560 --> 23:24.980
Oh, that makes so much sense.

23:25.240 --> 23:25.460
Right.

23:25.820 --> 23:28.420
A bird does not perceive the chair as sit-able.

23:28.660 --> 23:30.020
It perceives it as perch-able.

23:30.580 --> 23:35.640
The perception is defined by the organism's body, not by a neutral internal map.

23:35.640 --> 23:36.940
Okay, that is so clear.

23:36.940 --> 23:49.060
If you throw a heavy medicine ball at my face, I don't calculate the Bayesian probability of a spherical object intersecting with my nasal cavity, and I don't calculate how to minimize the free energy of that sensory event.

23:49.220 --> 23:49.840
I hope not.

23:50.220 --> 23:52.660
My body perceives duckability, and I just duck.

23:52.840 --> 23:54.760
The action is built right into the perception.

23:54.760 --> 23:58.840
But wait, how do the predictive coding people defend their turf against this?

23:59.520 --> 24:03.280
Because when you lay it out like that, Brett's critique seems fatal.

24:03.740 --> 24:06.460
It seems so obvious that we aren't just calculating pixels.

24:06.800 --> 24:11.040
Well, they defend it by doing what theoretical physicists sometimes do when a theory is threatened.

24:11.540 --> 24:14.020
They expand the math even further to swallow the critique.

24:14.420 --> 24:15.260
Ah, of course.

24:15.260 --> 24:20.360
Which leads to the next massive, perhaps fatal, problem Flyxion identifies.

24:21.060 --> 24:30.640
When you point out to a predictive processing theorist that organisms do things to survive, to maintain their temperature, to find food, not just to predict sensory inputs.

24:30.740 --> 24:33.720
They say, ah, but survival is just a form of prediction.

24:34.400 --> 24:34.700
What?

24:34.940 --> 24:38.480
This is the grand unifying theory of active inference.

24:38.680 --> 24:39.300
Okay, yes.

24:39.760 --> 24:40.460
Active inference.

24:40.460 --> 24:48.240
This is where the whole minimize surprise framework is taken to its absolute wildest logical extreme.

24:48.480 --> 24:48.800
It does.

24:49.140 --> 24:51.160
Under active inference, they try to unify everything.

24:51.520 --> 24:53.760
Perception is updating your brain to match the world.

24:54.140 --> 24:56.300
Action is updating the world to match your brain.

24:56.460 --> 24:57.520
Okay, break that down for me.

24:57.580 --> 24:59.860
How does action update the world to match my brain?

25:00.140 --> 25:06.300
The theory posits that you have hardwired priors, deep evolutionary predictions about your preferred states.

25:06.300 --> 25:11.540
For example, your brain strongly predicts that you have a core body temperature of 98.6 degrees.

25:12.120 --> 25:14.120
If you walk outside in the winter, you get cold.

25:14.240 --> 25:16.160
You experience a massive prediction error.

25:16.500 --> 25:20.100
Your sensory input cold does not match your prior prediction warm.

25:20.360 --> 25:21.120
So what do you do?

25:21.200 --> 25:21.960
You put on a sweater.

25:22.400 --> 25:28.020
The act of putting on a sweater changes your sensory input so that it fulfills your prediction that you will be warm.

25:28.020 --> 25:37.940
Therefore, according to active inference, absolutely all biological action is just a mechanism for fulfilling your own sensory predictions to minimize free energy.

25:38.300 --> 25:41.800
I mean, I have to admit, it sounds incredibly elegant on paper.

25:42.500 --> 25:46.440
It unites seeing and doing under one grand mathematical umbrella.

25:46.900 --> 25:48.200
It's the theory of everything.

25:48.360 --> 25:49.300
What's the problem with it?

25:49.300 --> 26:00.020
The problem is that to make that math work, it has to completely collapse fundamentally different biological realities into a single, meaningless mathematical currency.

26:00.180 --> 26:01.000
Meaningless currency.

26:01.280 --> 26:08.280
Specifically, Flyxion points out that active inference collapses constraints, preferences, and goals into the exact same variable.

26:08.360 --> 26:08.720
Okay, wait.

26:08.960 --> 26:12.860
Let's define those three things for the listener because this seems like the crux of the biological argument.

26:13.280 --> 26:16.840
What is the difference between a constraint, a preference, and a goal?

26:16.840 --> 26:21.440
A constraint is a hard, non-negotiable limit on what keeps you alive.

26:21.980 --> 26:24.680
If you start breathing oxygen for five minutes, you die.

26:24.960 --> 26:28.300
If your internal body temperature drops below 70 degrees, you die.

26:28.480 --> 26:30.680
That is a hard biological constraint.

26:31.000 --> 26:31.780
Okay, got it.

26:31.840 --> 26:32.620
What about a preference?

26:33.060 --> 26:37.040
A preference is what you naturally tend toward when you aren't fighting for your life.

26:37.480 --> 26:43.480
Like preferring to sit in a plush, comfortable armchair rather than a hard wooden stool.

26:43.900 --> 26:46.520
You won't die on the stool, but you prefer the armchair.

26:46.520 --> 26:47.160
Makes sense.

26:47.340 --> 26:47.780
And a goal.

26:48.100 --> 26:56.520
A goal is a specific, agentic target you are aiming for in the future, like deciding to run a marathon or finishing writing a novel.

26:56.740 --> 27:06.040
An active inference says all three of those things, needing oxygen, liking soft chairs, and wanting to write a book, are all just prior predictions trying to minimize error.

27:06.160 --> 27:06.460
Yes.

27:07.020 --> 27:13.960
And if a single mathematical scalar value has to represent all of those things, it completely loses its explanatory power.

27:14.120 --> 27:14.580
How so?

27:14.580 --> 27:20.240
Let's say you predict that you are going to win an Olympic gold medal, and you also predict that it might rain tomorrow afternoon.

27:20.920 --> 27:24.760
Active inference uses the exact same mathematical equation for both.

27:25.040 --> 27:25.560
Oh, I see.

27:25.560 --> 27:36.480
It cannot easily distinguish between the deeply held prediction that forms your core identity and drives you to train in the gym for four years, and the passive prediction that just notes a weather forecast.

27:37.220 --> 27:40.580
One is an active, world-shaping goal that defines your agency.

27:41.180 --> 27:42.640
The other is passive observation.

27:42.640 --> 27:49.100
By labeling them both as predictions, we lose the very essence of what biological agency actually is.

27:49.100 --> 27:55.560
Here is where it gets really interesting for me, and I want you, the listener, to think about this like a video game.

27:55.680 --> 27:56.740
Oh, good analogy.

27:57.060 --> 28:05.640
In a standard machine learning model, or in this active inference theory, minimizing prediction error is like trying to maximize a single score in an arcade game.

28:05.640 --> 28:12.720
Every action you take, every move you make, goes toward making that one single number go up, or in the case of error, go down to zero.

28:13.220 --> 28:14.440
It's an optimization game.

28:14.640 --> 28:14.840
Right.

28:14.840 --> 28:20.200
But, biological life isn't about maximizing a score, it's about not turning off the console.

28:20.640 --> 28:22.920
It's about staying in the game at all costs.

28:23.380 --> 28:26.820
And friction provides an actual, rigorous mathematical proof for this, right?

28:27.480 --> 28:28.560
Theorem 2.2.

28:28.900 --> 28:29.220
Yes.

28:29.640 --> 28:35.280
Theorem 2.2 in the text is a brilliant mathematical formalization of this exact intuition.

28:35.280 --> 28:47.900
Flyxion proves that no continuous scalar loss function, meaning no single mathematical number you try to smoothly minimize over time, can accurately represent hard biological constraints.

28:48.160 --> 28:49.620
Walk me through the math on that.

28:49.700 --> 28:52.900
Why can't I just program an infinite penalty into a loss function?

28:53.320 --> 28:57.500
Why can't the math just say, if you run out of oxygen, error equals infinity?

28:57.980 --> 29:00.720
Because of how the optimization math actually works.

29:00.720 --> 29:04.980
The math used in these predictive models relies on gradient descent.

29:04.980 --> 29:06.160
It relies on calculus.

29:06.620 --> 29:06.680
Okay.

29:07.020 --> 29:10.900
Calculus requires smooth, continuous curves to find the bottom of a valley.

29:11.580 --> 29:15.480
Imagine you are blindfolded, walking down a hill, trying to find the lowest point.

29:16.020 --> 29:19.700
You take a step, feel which way the slope goes down, and take another step in that direction.

29:20.080 --> 29:21.180
That is gradient descent.

29:21.300 --> 29:21.540
Right.

29:21.660 --> 29:22.760
It's a smooth optimization.

29:23.160 --> 29:25.720
But a hard biological constraint is not a slope.

29:25.820 --> 29:27.880
It is a sheer, discontinuous cliff.

29:28.260 --> 29:30.160
If you step over the line, you instantly die.

29:30.620 --> 29:31.320
The game is over.

29:31.340 --> 29:31.780
Oh, wow.

29:31.780 --> 29:37.240
If you are walking blindfolded using gradient descent, the slope leading right up to a thousand

29:37.240 --> 29:39.280
foot cliff might be perfectly gentle.

29:39.960 --> 29:42.060
The math tells you to keep stepping forward.

29:42.320 --> 29:46.560
It cannot see the discontinuity until you have already stepped off and ceased to exist.

29:46.800 --> 29:49.080
Because it only looks at the immediate local slope.

29:49.180 --> 29:49.680
Exactly.

29:50.040 --> 29:50.220
Yeah.

29:50.220 --> 29:56.180
You cannot use a smooth, differentiable loss function to mathematically describe if I take

29:56.180 --> 29:58.880
one more step, my system instantly dissolves.

29:59.360 --> 30:04.760
Therefore, optimizing a single number cannot possibly be the underlying architecture of living

30:04.760 --> 30:05.300
things.

30:05.520 --> 30:06.560
We don't optimize.

30:06.960 --> 30:07.700
We navigate.

30:08.100 --> 30:08.360
Boom.

30:08.360 --> 30:10.740
Okay, so we've torn down the prediction machine.

30:11.200 --> 30:15.040
We've established that the math fundamentally does not work for life or death biological

30:15.040 --> 30:15.640
survival.

30:16.080 --> 30:20.700
If the brain isn't a prediction error minimizer running gradient descent, what is it actually

30:20.700 --> 30:20.960
doing?

30:21.160 --> 30:26.020
This brings us directly to Flyxion's radical alternative, the admissibility framework.

30:26.160 --> 30:26.720
Admissibility.

30:27.020 --> 30:27.340
Yes.

30:27.860 --> 30:33.440
This is where the paper moves from a devastating critique to a totally new constructive paradigm.

30:33.440 --> 30:37.640
Flyxion proposes that cognition is not about statistical inference.

30:38.180 --> 30:39.780
It's not about guessing what's out there.

30:40.140 --> 30:41.680
Cognition is about navigation.

30:42.460 --> 30:47.080
Specifically, navigating something mathematically defined as admissibility.

30:47.540 --> 30:48.140
Admissibility.

30:48.740 --> 30:50.800
Mathematically denoted as A of T.

30:51.240 --> 30:53.340
What does that actually look like in plain English?

30:53.620 --> 30:59.120
Imagine your life, your existence, as a vast, high-dimensional landscape of possible paths

30:59.120 --> 30:59.700
you could take.

30:59.700 --> 31:04.980
Every choice you make, every movement of your arm, every cellular metabolic process happening

31:04.980 --> 31:06.220
inside you right now.

31:06.300 --> 31:07.300
All the possible futures.

31:07.440 --> 31:07.840
Exactly.

31:08.560 --> 31:12.760
Some of those paths lead to you staying alive, maintaining your identity, and keeping your

31:12.760 --> 31:14.260
biological organization intact.

31:14.800 --> 31:18.360
Some paths lead to injury, systemic collapse, disease, or death.

31:18.620 --> 31:24.000
The admissible subspace A of T is the geometric collection of all dynamically possible paths

31:24.000 --> 31:25.880
that keep you viable as a living system.

31:25.880 --> 31:30.000
So cognition thinking, acting, perceiving, is really just the act of steering your ship

31:30.000 --> 31:32.580
to stay within those safe, viable paths over time.

31:32.800 --> 31:33.200
Exactly.

31:33.580 --> 31:36.120
Let's look at the profound shift in priorities here.

31:36.980 --> 31:42.320
Under the predictive framework, your brain's main job is to reduce epistemic uncertainty.

31:43.060 --> 31:45.700
It desperately wants to be right about the world.

31:45.840 --> 31:50.040
It wants its internal model to perfectly match the outside reality.

31:50.440 --> 31:50.880
And under admissibility.

31:51.580 --> 31:55.040
Under the admissibility framework, your brain does not care about being right.

31:55.040 --> 31:59.120
Its main job is to remain a viable breathing system.

31:59.980 --> 32:03.440
It prioritizes what Flyxion calls coherence over correspondence.

32:03.800 --> 32:05.220
Coherence over correspondence.

32:05.540 --> 32:08.020
I feel like this is the master key to the whole paper.

32:08.120 --> 32:09.500
Let's really impact those two words.

32:10.000 --> 32:12.400
Correspondence is about literal accuracy.

32:13.580 --> 32:16.840
Does your internal map strictly correspond to the external territory?

32:17.060 --> 32:19.500
A digital thermostat optimizes correspondence.

32:19.760 --> 32:22.440
It tracks the exact temperature of the room to the decimal point.

32:22.660 --> 32:22.760
Right.

32:22.760 --> 32:25.400
A large language model optimizes correspondence.

32:26.000 --> 32:31.240
It is trained to generate text that statistically perfectly corresponds to the vast data set it was trained on.

32:31.360 --> 32:34.960
But neither a thermostat nor a server rack running an LLM is alive.

32:35.400 --> 32:35.740
Right.

32:36.020 --> 32:38.000
Because they don't have to worry about coherence.

32:38.540 --> 32:44.960
Coherence is the unique ability of a living system to maintain its complex, fragile internal structure

32:44.960 --> 32:48.840
despite constant violent shocks and changes from the outside world.

32:48.840 --> 32:52.280
It's what Flyxion calls homeodynamic organization.

32:52.880 --> 32:54.160
Homeodynamic organization.

32:54.380 --> 32:55.040
Think about it.

32:55.400 --> 32:57.380
You are constantly being perturbed.

32:57.620 --> 32:58.580
The temperature drops.

32:58.820 --> 32:59.440
You get hungry.

32:59.620 --> 33:00.840
A loud noise startles you.

33:00.920 --> 33:01.880
You get stressed at work.

33:01.980 --> 33:03.580
But you constantly recover.

33:04.080 --> 33:08.640
Your internal systems, your heart rate, your metabolism, your sense of self,

33:08.640 --> 33:12.020
all work dynamically together to keep you from falling apart.

33:12.400 --> 33:13.940
That is coherence.

33:14.280 --> 33:22.360
And what Flyxion argues is that living beings will happily, aggressively throw correspondence out the window if it helps them maintain coherence.

33:22.600 --> 33:28.220
They will choose to be completely wrong about reality if being wrong keeps them from falling apart.

33:28.220 --> 33:28.980
Precisely.

33:29.140 --> 33:31.780
And this flips all of modern psychology on its head.

33:31.780 --> 33:38.380
From a predictive coding standpoint, things like cognitive biases, motivated reasoning, or distorting your own memories,

33:38.820 --> 33:40.600
these are viewed as bugs in the system.

33:40.700 --> 33:40.860
Right.

33:40.960 --> 33:41.640
They're irrational.

33:41.860 --> 33:44.640
There are irrational failures of the prediction machine.

33:45.120 --> 33:49.360
But from an admissibility standpoint, they are absolutely essential features.

33:49.800 --> 33:53.900
I want you, the listener, to think about this for yourself right now.

33:53.900 --> 34:03.680
Have you ever ignored a hard, obvious truth because accepting it would completely blow up your life, your marriage, or your core identity?

34:03.880 --> 34:04.520
We all have.

34:04.640 --> 34:11.720
Have you ever rationalized a terrible mistake you made because the literal guilt would be too paralyzing for you to get out of bed and function?

34:12.440 --> 34:13.640
That is not your brain glitching.

34:13.700 --> 34:14.860
That is not a failure of logic.

34:14.960 --> 34:16.580
That is your brain functioning perfectly.

34:16.580 --> 34:27.600
It is prioritizing your admissible trajectory, keeping you moving forward, keeping you biologically and psychologically coherent over building an accurate, corresponding world model.

34:27.880 --> 34:29.160
It is a profound realization.

34:30.120 --> 34:33.580
We willingly, structurally sacrifice truth to maintain our organizational stability.

34:34.320 --> 34:38.780
Because if your organizational stability collapses, you drop out of the admissible space entirely.

34:39.220 --> 34:40.220
You cease to function.

34:40.640 --> 34:45.780
And this brings us, naturally, to how Flyxion completely redefines mental health and pathology.

34:45.780 --> 34:55.440
If cognition is the geometry of navigating viable paths, we have to ask, what happens to the math when those paths start to shrink?

34:55.560 --> 34:57.800
Yes, let's talk about the geometry of pathology.

34:58.100 --> 35:00.520
Because this part of the paper blew my mind.

35:00.580 --> 35:05.480
Under the old inferential model, mental illness is framed as a computational error.

35:05.920 --> 35:06.180
Right.

35:06.800 --> 35:14.100
Depression or anxiety are seen as having inappropriate priors or giving too much statistical weight to negative prediction errors.

35:14.100 --> 35:18.820
You hold false beliefs about your self-worth or the danger of the world.

35:19.220 --> 35:21.380
Which, honestly, always felt a bit insulting.

35:21.880 --> 35:32.500
If you are in the middle of a crippling depressive episode and someone tells you, oh, you just have a bad statistical model of reality, you just need to update your priors, it completely misses the visceral reality of the pain.

35:32.640 --> 35:33.060
Exactly.

35:33.340 --> 35:36.760
It pathologizes the logic rather than the lived reality.

35:37.540 --> 35:39.800
Flyxion redefines pathology geometrically.

35:40.320 --> 35:42.440
Let's look at depression through the lens of admissibility.

35:42.440 --> 35:42.960
Okay.

35:43.160 --> 35:44.300
It is not a false belief.

35:44.700 --> 35:49.080
Depression is a literal, physical, and psychological contraction of your admissible subspace.

35:49.280 --> 35:50.000
A contraction.

35:50.400 --> 35:55.900
The topology, the shape of what is possible for you, physically narrows.

35:57.000 --> 36:07.480
Actions that used to be easy and available, getting out of bed, calling a friend, going for a walk, enjoying a meal, are no longer geometrically available to you in that state.

36:07.820 --> 36:09.560
Your trajectory space has collapsed.

36:09.560 --> 36:18.120
It's like you're standing in a room, and the walls are literally closing in, and the doors you used to walk through are just vanishing into solid brick.

36:18.360 --> 36:19.600
That's exactly how it feels.

36:19.740 --> 36:22.100
Purpose of action becomes geometrically unavailable.

36:22.360 --> 36:24.720
It's not that you're predicting you'll fail to call a friend.

36:25.280 --> 36:28.660
It's that the past of the phone doesn't exist in your current state.

36:29.400 --> 36:31.980
That is so much more empathetic to the reality of depression.

36:32.120 --> 36:32.980
It really is.

36:33.040 --> 36:33.660
What about addiction?

36:33.800 --> 36:35.060
How does geometry explain that?

36:35.400 --> 36:38.360
Addiction is framed as a local stabilization loop.

36:39.080 --> 36:43.180
Think of your healthy life as a wide, rolling, exploratory landscape.

36:43.580 --> 36:44.800
You can go in many directions.

36:44.920 --> 36:45.120
Right.

36:45.380 --> 36:49.800
Addiction is a deep, narrow pit in that landscape, an attractor basin.

36:49.800 --> 36:57.600
When you fall into it, the drug or the gambling or the behavior provides intense, immediate local stability.

36:58.100 --> 37:01.040
It heavily regulates your nervous system in the short term.

37:01.140 --> 37:03.000
It makes you feel coherent right now.

37:03.380 --> 37:04.500
But it destroys everything else.

37:04.700 --> 37:04.960
Yes.

37:05.460 --> 37:08.060
It dominates your global coherence.

37:08.060 --> 37:15.520
You get stuck in this tiny, tight loop that feels locally safe, but it destroys your ability to navigate the larger landscape of your life.

37:15.720 --> 37:18.740
It cuts you off from your relationships, your career, your health.

37:18.920 --> 37:22.660
It destroys your long-term viability for the sake of short-term stabilization.

37:23.380 --> 37:24.740
So you have perfect local prediction.

37:25.420 --> 37:30.800
You know exactly how that drink or that pill will make you feel right now, but you suffer total global collapse.

37:30.980 --> 37:31.420
Exactly.

37:31.420 --> 37:42.140
And to explain how we move between these different states, how we go from healthy to depressed or healthy to addicted Flyxion, introduces this concept of stratified admissibility.

37:42.460 --> 37:44.000
This is a vital piece of the puzzle.

37:44.440 --> 37:48.180
The admissible space your life landscape isn't just one smooth, flat plane.

37:48.360 --> 37:49.880
It has strata or levels.

37:49.900 --> 37:50.940
Like a multi-story building.

37:51.200 --> 37:51.640
Exactly.

37:52.280 --> 37:54.700
Being awake is a different stratum than being asleep.

37:55.060 --> 38:01.180
Being highly focused and in a flow state is a different stratum than being completely overwhelmed by a panic attack.

38:01.420 --> 38:07.740
And Flyxion points out a fundamental mathematical asymmetry in how we move between these strata.

38:08.060 --> 38:10.500
Asymmetry meaning it's easier to go one way than the other, right?

38:10.520 --> 38:12.200
Yes, significantly easier.

38:12.420 --> 38:16.020
It is dynamically very easy to fall down a stratum.

38:16.100 --> 38:17.600
Think of it like thermodynamics.

38:18.340 --> 38:25.180
Stress, physical trauma, a major injury, severe sleep deprivation, resource depletion.

38:25.860 --> 38:33.680
These shocks can instantly break your current coherence and push you down into a lower organizational stratum, like a depressive state or a shock state.

38:34.040 --> 38:35.020
Which brings me to a question.

38:35.080 --> 38:37.080
Is this what we mean when we say someone hit rock bottom?

38:37.540 --> 38:46.400
Is hitting rock bottom literally falling into the lowest geometric stratum where all your previous attractors, your hobbies, your career, the things that used to make you happy no longer even exist in that space?

38:46.800 --> 38:47.860
That is exactly it.

38:47.860 --> 38:52.580
When you fall into a lower stratum, the actual boundary conditions of your reality change.

38:53.080 --> 38:55.260
The mathematical shape of your world changes.

38:55.840 --> 39:02.760
The attractors, the things that motivate you in a healthy state literally do not exist in the geometry of the lower stratum.

39:02.760 --> 39:03.200
Wow.

39:03.380 --> 39:07.420
That's why you can't just tell someone with severe depression to think positive or just go for a run.

39:07.500 --> 39:12.280
Because the positive thoughts exist on floor five, and they are trapped in the basement, and the stairs are gone.

39:12.440 --> 39:12.900
Exactly.

39:13.320 --> 39:16.100
And climbing back up is profoundly asymmetrical.

39:16.300 --> 39:18.060
You cannot just reverse the fall.

39:18.500 --> 39:20.880
If you break a vase, you can't just undrop it.

39:21.660 --> 39:25.920
Climbing back up to a higher stratum requires reconstructing the boundary conditions.

39:25.920 --> 39:33.920
It requires immense metabolic and cognitive energy, and crucially, it almost always requires external scaffolding.

39:34.000 --> 39:36.360
External scaffolding, meaning you can't do it alone.

39:36.580 --> 39:37.040
Rarely.

39:37.680 --> 39:44.660
Therapy, medication, a highly structured daily routine, deeply supportive relationships, a radical change in your physical environment.

39:45.160 --> 39:52.280
These act as external forces that help physically and psychologically rebuild the geometry of your world so you can eventually access the higher stratum again.

39:52.280 --> 39:57.340
This geometric view of collapse and rigidity, it scales up too, doesn't it?

39:57.480 --> 39:57.640
Yeah.

39:57.720 --> 39:59.700
You mentioned external scaffolding for a person.

40:00.460 --> 40:03.420
What happens when whole institutions fall down a stratum?

40:04.040 --> 40:06.440
Because we see broken systems all the time.

40:06.580 --> 40:06.840
Yes.

40:07.160 --> 40:10.120
The topological view applies perfectly to human institutions.

40:10.540 --> 40:11.920
Think of bureaucratic rigidity.

40:12.580 --> 40:15.860
That is an institutional trajectory collapse.

40:16.360 --> 40:17.560
Tell me more about that.

40:17.560 --> 40:27.380
Well, an organization, say, a government agency or a massive corporation starts out flexible, but then they encounter a problem, so they create a strict rule to solve it.

40:27.800 --> 40:29.380
Then another problem, another rule.

40:29.500 --> 40:30.780
Classic corporate bloke.

40:30.840 --> 40:35.940
Over decades, the system becomes completely over-constrained by its own local stabilization loops.

40:36.460 --> 40:38.480
Every action requires five signatures.

40:39.020 --> 40:42.780
It becomes a rigid bureaucracy that can no longer adapt to the outside environment.

40:42.980 --> 40:43.200
Right.

40:43.200 --> 40:53.100
Its admissible space has shrunk to a pinpoint of rule-following, and when the environment inevitably shifts, the institution collapses because it geometrically cannot navigate.

40:53.380 --> 40:53.860
Man.

40:54.520 --> 40:56.640
That explains so much about the modern world.

40:57.480 --> 40:57.660
Okay.

40:57.860 --> 41:06.460
So if falling down a stratum destroys your trajectory, how do healthy systems, how do healthy people preserve their trajectories across time?

41:06.640 --> 41:07.600
That's the real trick.

41:07.600 --> 41:10.360
Because, think about it, I go to sleep every night.

41:10.940 --> 41:13.100
For eight hours, my consciousness goes dark.

41:13.420 --> 41:14.940
I get distracted by my phone.

41:15.100 --> 41:16.160
I lose my train of thought.

41:16.480 --> 41:18.500
My trajectory is constantly being interrupted.

41:19.080 --> 41:20.700
How do I wake up and still be me?

41:21.160 --> 41:24.420
According to Flyxion, we survive these gaps by using clipboards.

41:24.860 --> 41:30.740
This is perhaps one of the most paradigm-shifting, beautiful parts of the entire paper, the clipboard thesis.

41:31.540 --> 41:37.100
But to understand it, it requires us to radically, completely redefine what human memory actually is.

41:37.100 --> 41:37.400
Right.

41:37.500 --> 41:40.360
Because if the brain isn't a computer, then memory isn't a hard drive.

41:40.460 --> 41:41.060
It's not RAM.

41:41.760 --> 41:50.320
If you view memory as a storage drive, you think you are filing away little video clips of your life in your hippocampus, and when you want to remember something, you just search for the file and hit play.

41:50.400 --> 41:52.520
Like pulling up an MP4 on your desktop.

41:52.780 --> 41:54.020
Flyxion says absolutely not.

41:54.180 --> 41:55.860
Memory is not a storage of content.

41:56.380 --> 41:58.500
Memory is a stabilization operator.

41:58.940 --> 41:59.960
The clipboard thesis.

42:00.660 --> 42:02.280
Think about the clipboard function on your computer.

42:02.280 --> 42:10.100
You highlight some text, you hit copy, and that text floats in this invisible, suspended state until you go somewhere else and hit paste.

42:10.280 --> 42:10.880
Exactly.

42:11.380 --> 42:15.680
Flyxion takes that digital metaphor and explodes it into the physical world.

42:15.680 --> 42:22.020
When you sleep or when you are deeply distracted by a loud noise, your cognitive trajectory is paused.

42:22.720 --> 42:24.780
You lose the immediate threat of your action.

42:25.240 --> 42:31.860
A stabilization operator is anything internal or external that preserves your coherence across that temporal gap.

42:32.100 --> 42:32.720
That's amazing.

42:32.720 --> 42:37.660
And what's fascinating here is that Flyxion extends this far beyond the neurons in your brain.

42:38.300 --> 42:49.480
Symbolic structures, the handwritten notes on your desk, the essays you read, mathematical proofs, even cultural rituals and religious ceremonies, they do not store content like a flash drive.

42:49.620 --> 42:52.040
Wait, if a note doesn't store content, what is it doing?

42:52.340 --> 42:55.140
When I write down a grocery list, I'm storing content, aren't I?

42:55.360 --> 42:57.460
No, you are storing a constraint structure.

42:57.600 --> 42:58.480
A constraint structure.

42:58.660 --> 42:59.360
Let me explain.

42:59.360 --> 43:06.580
When you read a profound essay by a brilliant thinker, you aren't just downloading data packets into your brain.

43:06.920 --> 43:10.620
The physical words on the page act as a topological glue.

43:11.100 --> 43:13.540
It creates a highly specific set of constraints.

43:14.140 --> 43:14.840
Okay, I'm tracking.

43:15.100 --> 43:21.280
As you read it, the structure of the language forces your mind to navigate through a specific geometry of thought.

43:21.700 --> 43:27.380
It forces you to regenerate the organizational state of the author inside your own biology.

43:27.380 --> 43:36.240
The essay is a stabilization operator that allows someone else's trajectory to be physically reconstructed in your mind across time and space.

43:36.660 --> 43:37.520
I absolutely love this.

43:37.580 --> 43:39.220
So for the listener, let me give you an analogy.

43:39.720 --> 43:41.700
Tell me if this works better than the jet engine one.

43:41.780 --> 43:42.060
Hit me.

43:42.400 --> 43:45.680
It's like a save point in a massive open world video game.

43:45.680 --> 44:01.360
But instead of saving the exact location of every single pixel on the screen, the save point just saves the core physics engine rules and the boundary conditions of that specific level, so your console can completely reconstruct the environment from scratch when you boot it up tomorrow.

44:01.540 --> 44:02.760
That is exactly what it's like.

44:02.760 --> 44:08.740
Does that mean our physical environments, our messy desks, our carefully decorated living rooms?

44:09.020 --> 44:16.660
The specific way we arrange our books, are they acting as literal external stabilization operators for our identity?

44:17.200 --> 44:17.760
Unquestionably.

44:18.100 --> 44:19.560
They are part of your cognitive system.

44:19.880 --> 44:29.440
You arrange your desk in a highly specific way so that tomorrow, when you sit down and look at it, the physical environment constrains your trajectory back into a working state.

44:29.440 --> 44:29.920
Wow.

44:30.180 --> 44:34.160
Your environment is quite literally holding your cognitive state together.

44:34.540 --> 44:44.300
If someone comes in and entirely rearranges your office, you feel deeply disoriented, not just because you can't find a pen, but because your external stabilization operators have been destroyed.

44:44.600 --> 44:47.300
You have lost a piece of your cognitive geometry.

44:47.540 --> 44:50.680
And this leads directly to how Flyxion defines selfhood.

44:51.040 --> 44:51.400
Yes.

44:51.640 --> 44:53.020
Who are you, mathematically?

44:53.340 --> 44:55.700
The self is not a persistent mental substance.

44:55.700 --> 44:59.420
You do not have a little glowing soul pearl sitting in your pineal gland.

44:59.660 --> 45:04.860
The self is defined as the persistence of a characteristic constraint structure through time.

45:05.160 --> 45:07.500
The persistence of a characteristic constraint structure.

45:07.680 --> 45:08.000
Yes.

45:08.500 --> 45:16.480
To understand this, we can look at philosophers of temporality, like Henri Bergson, Alfred North Whitehead, or Gilbert Simondon.

45:16.920 --> 45:24.080
They argued for decades that time isn't just a universal parameter we move through, like a train moving on a prelaid track.

45:24.080 --> 45:29.580
Living organisms constitute their own temporal horizons through their ongoing activity.

45:30.320 --> 45:32.720
Time is something we generate through our persistence.

45:33.320 --> 45:33.420
Right.

45:33.500 --> 45:36.820
Time is something we make, not just something we passively experience.

45:37.180 --> 45:39.980
And the stories we tell is crucial here.

45:40.340 --> 45:44.160
The story you tell about yourself, I am a creative artist.

45:44.560 --> 45:45.900
I am a resilient survivor.

45:46.140 --> 45:47.300
I am a reliable friend.

45:47.700 --> 45:50.960
That story is not a historical record in a filing cabinet.

45:51.420 --> 45:53.220
It is a living constraint map.

45:53.220 --> 45:54.020
A living man.

45:54.080 --> 46:02.540
By continually telling yourself and others that narrative, you make certain life trajectories geometrically available to you, and you make other trajectories unavailable.

46:02.760 --> 46:03.740
Let's give an example of that.

46:03.960 --> 46:11.540
If you view yourself as a dedicated athlete, the trajectory of waking up at 5 a.m. and the freezing cold to run becomes admissible.

46:12.100 --> 46:14.140
It's hard, but it fits the geometry.

46:14.140 --> 46:19.360
The trajectory of eating junk food on the couch all day becomes geometrically inadmissible.

46:19.920 --> 46:21.520
It violates the constraint structure.

46:21.980 --> 46:24.640
Your narrative physically stabilizes your selfhood.

46:24.640 --> 46:31.120
So the self is this global, continuous, topological map of constraints that we are constantly regenerating.

46:31.300 --> 46:33.540
But wait, let me put all my skeptics hat again.

46:33.560 --> 46:34.160
Okay, let's have it.

46:34.580 --> 46:41.120
I am fully on board with the geometry of selfhood, but I cannot deny that I do make local predictions all the time.

46:41.660 --> 46:48.500
When I play catch and someone throws a ball, my brain is clearly doing some kind of physics calculation of where the ball will be so my hand can meet it.

46:48.500 --> 46:49.120
Of course.

46:49.260 --> 46:56.660
When I listen to a comedian tell a joke, my brain anticipates the punchline, and the sudden violation of that prediction is exactly why it's funny.

46:57.480 --> 47:04.800
How do these very obvious, highly effective local predictions fit into a globally continuous topological self?

47:05.140 --> 47:09.060
It is a great question, and it brings us to the mathematical heart of the paper.

47:09.760 --> 47:12.260
Flyxion does not deny that prediction exists.

47:12.560 --> 47:14.640
They aren't throwing the baby out with the bathwater.

47:14.740 --> 47:17.540
They are just drastically demoting prediction.

47:17.840 --> 47:18.440
Demoting it.

47:18.640 --> 47:22.620
Prediction is a subordinate local regulatory mechanism.

47:23.280 --> 47:24.780
It is not the CEO of the brain.

47:24.900 --> 47:25.940
It is middle management.

47:26.240 --> 47:27.280
Okay, middle management.

47:27.620 --> 47:31.020
And to explain this, Flyxion brings in some extremely heavy math.

47:31.780 --> 47:33.800
Lie groups versus lie algebra.

47:34.500 --> 47:35.780
I'll be completely honest.

47:35.840 --> 47:38.940
When I read this section, the math was so dense I had to read it three times.

47:39.320 --> 47:40.580
Can you walk us through this slowly?

47:40.720 --> 47:41.440
What is a lie group?

47:41.740 --> 47:42.360
I can.

47:42.360 --> 47:45.620
It is a beautiful mathematical analogy once you visualize it.

47:45.620 --> 47:50.640
A lie group is a complex, globally curved, geometric object, a manifold.

47:51.000 --> 47:53.680
Think of a perfect massive sphere like the globe of the Earth.

47:53.700 --> 47:54.680
Okay, I have a globe in my head.

47:54.680 --> 48:05.600
In Flyxion's framework, this massive curved sphere represents the global organizational structure of an organism over its entire lifespan, its total viability space.

48:05.600 --> 48:15.040
Now, if you are a tiny ant standing on the surface of the Earth and you zoom in infinitely close to just the one square foot you are standing on, it doesn't look curved.

48:15.160 --> 48:16.360
It looks completely flat.

48:16.460 --> 48:16.600
Right.

48:16.720 --> 48:17.660
The flat Earth illusion.

48:17.660 --> 48:18.260
Exactly.

48:18.480 --> 48:24.800
That flat, localized, lineal space that touches the curve at exactly one point is called the tangent space.

48:25.380 --> 48:27.120
Mathematically, that is the lie algebra.

48:27.480 --> 48:34.020
So the lie group is the whole massive curved globe, and the lie algebra is the tiny flat map of your immediate local neighborhood.

48:34.120 --> 48:34.420
Yes.

48:34.880 --> 48:39.600
Flyxion uses a specific algebraic structure, the SL2 seed structure, to illustrate this.

48:40.040 --> 48:44.600
This algebra operates using three basic generators, H, E, and F.

48:44.900 --> 48:45.780
H, E, and F.

48:45.780 --> 48:47.960
H represents your current immediate state.

48:48.520 --> 48:51.140
E is an operator that elevates or abstracts your state.

48:51.480 --> 48:53.080
It opens up new local possibilities.

48:53.580 --> 48:55.340
F lowers or consolidates your state.

48:55.400 --> 48:56.340
It locks things down.

48:56.340 --> 49:00.700
By continuously applying E and F, you navigate your immediate flat local space.

49:01.160 --> 49:05.180
So these generators, these are the local rules, the local prediction algorithms.

49:05.420 --> 49:05.680
Yes.

49:06.000 --> 49:10.960
And the crucial devastating point Flyxion makes against predictive processing is this.

49:10.960 --> 49:21.560
Just because you perfectly understand the math of the local tangent space, the lie algebra, the local prediction mechanism does not mean you understand the global topology of the lie group.

49:21.700 --> 49:22.200
Oh, wow.

49:22.360 --> 49:36.080
Knowing exactly how a neural circuit minimizes local prediction error in the visual cortex when catching a ball tells you absolutely nothing about how the organism persists as a unified, coherent self over 80 years of life.

49:36.080 --> 49:40.200
Okay, let me try to translate this math into a physical situation to make sure I really have it.

49:40.260 --> 49:40.700
I'll for it.

49:41.200 --> 49:48.860
If I'm driving a car through the Rocky Mountains in the dead of night, the lie algebra, the local prediction mechanism, is my steering wheel and my headlights.

49:49.400 --> 49:54.420
The headlights are illuminating a tiny, flat, linear patch of asphalt 10 feet in front of me.

49:54.420 --> 49:58.500
I am constantly micro-correcting the steering wheel to stay on that 10-foot patch.

49:58.600 --> 49:59.240
Perfect so far.

49:59.540 --> 50:07.340
But the lie group, the global topology, is the entire massive curved Rocky Mountain Range that I am navigating.

50:07.840 --> 50:18.140
What you're saying is predictive processing theorists are obsessively studying the headlights, studying the steering column, and boldly claiming that the mountain range itself does not exist.

50:18.620 --> 50:20.240
That is exactly what they are doing.

50:20.380 --> 50:22.600
They are confusing the headlights for the mountain.

50:22.600 --> 50:26.700
And this raises an incredibly important question about biology.

50:27.500 --> 50:34.560
Can you have excellent local prediction, perfect headlights, but terrible global admissibility?

50:34.800 --> 50:35.720
I would assume yes.

50:35.900 --> 50:37.500
The answer is absolutely yes.

50:37.820 --> 50:46.200
In fact, healthy biological systems constantly tolerate noisy, terrible local predictions specifically to preserve their global coherence.

50:46.640 --> 50:47.040
How so?

50:47.360 --> 50:48.780
Think about walking on a rocky trail.

50:49.200 --> 50:49.760
You will stumble.

50:49.920 --> 50:50.640
You will make mistakes.

50:50.640 --> 50:52.460
You will misjudge the distance of a rock.

50:52.740 --> 50:54.500
Your local prediction completely fails.

50:54.620 --> 50:57.580
But you throw your arms out, you keep your balance, and you stay alive.

50:57.720 --> 51:00.980
You sacrifice local accuracy to maintain global persistence.

51:01.260 --> 51:03.280
You tolerate the noise to keep the system running.

51:03.600 --> 51:05.620
But the inverse is much more dangerous.

51:06.340 --> 51:10.200
You can optimize locally so perfectly that you destroy yourself globally.

51:10.540 --> 51:11.000
Like what?

51:11.000 --> 51:12.740
Think of the biology of cancer.

51:12.740 --> 51:17.360
A cancer cell is optimizing its local growth and replication perfectly.

51:17.900 --> 51:20.540
It is succeeding wildly at the local cellular level.

51:21.080 --> 51:23.560
It is minimizing its own local errors.

51:23.680 --> 51:24.740
And it's killing the person.

51:24.880 --> 51:25.240
Exactly.

51:25.620 --> 51:28.740
It has lost all connection to the global coherence of the body.

51:29.100 --> 51:33.360
And its perfect local success eventually kills the host, thereby killing itself.

51:33.360 --> 51:37.580
Hyper-optimized human bureaucracies do the exact same thing.

51:37.580 --> 51:43.540
They optimize a local metric, like obsessively maximizing quarterly profits or rigidly enforcing

51:43.540 --> 51:44.880
a minor departmental rule.

51:45.220 --> 51:49.700
And in doing so, they destroy the long-term viability of the entire company.

51:49.800 --> 51:50.380
Okay, hold on.

51:50.500 --> 51:51.360
This is huge.

51:51.840 --> 51:57.760
Having incredibly sophisticated local prediction rules, but having absolutely zero global persistence

51:57.760 --> 52:02.840
and structure, optimizing a local statistical metric without having a physical body to keep

52:02.840 --> 52:04.580
alive, you're describing AI.

52:04.960 --> 52:07.220
You're describing modern large language models.

52:07.440 --> 52:07.700
Yes.

52:08.000 --> 52:12.920
We have arrived at the final and perhaps most urgent major insight of the paper, artificial

52:12.920 --> 52:15.240
intelligence and the absolute limits of prediction.

52:15.600 --> 52:17.100
This is where the rubber meets the road.

52:17.420 --> 52:22.380
Because right now, the entire tech industry is convinced that if we just make the predictive

52:22.380 --> 52:27.200
models bigger, if we just give them more data to minimize their loss functions, they

52:27.200 --> 52:28.940
will wake up and become conscious agents.

52:29.040 --> 52:29.840
I really believe that.

52:30.100 --> 52:36.280
But if Flyxion is right, everything we think about, ChatGPT, Claude, and all these LLMs is

52:36.280 --> 52:37.340
structurally skewed.

52:38.180 --> 52:43.840
Why do large language models appear so incredibly, genuinely intelligent to us?

52:43.840 --> 52:47.340
They appear intelligent because they are trained on our data.

52:47.840 --> 52:52.080
And human data, all the books, the heartbreaking poetry, the scientific essays, the millions of

52:52.080 --> 52:57.040
casual conversations scraped from the internet, that data is absolutely saturated with the traces

52:57.040 --> 53:00.700
of genuine human agency, purpose, and admissibility.

53:00.900 --> 53:01.860
The traces of it, yeah.

53:02.000 --> 53:06.860
Human language inherently encodes our biological constraints, our fears of death, our emotions,

53:06.860 --> 53:08.480
and our temporal continuity.

53:08.480 --> 53:12.640
So the LLM is just a giant, highly polished statistical mirror.

53:13.180 --> 53:13.480
Yes.

53:13.940 --> 53:16.900
Flyxion uses the hurricane analogy to explain this perfectly.

53:17.780 --> 53:23.340
Meteorologists can run a numerical simulation of a Category 5 hurricane on a massive supercomputer.

53:23.340 --> 53:26.780
The simulation is mathematically incredibly accurate.

53:27.180 --> 53:28.540
It predicts pressure gradients.

53:28.680 --> 53:29.780
It calculates wind speeds.

53:29.880 --> 53:31.700
It perfectly models where the storm will go.

53:32.100 --> 53:35.760
But the simulation inside the computer does not have a physical eye.

53:36.220 --> 53:39.240
It does not release latent heat into the atmosphere.

53:39.780 --> 53:42.600
And crucially, the simulation isn't wet.

53:43.120 --> 53:45.200
A simulated hurricane doesn't blow down your house.

53:45.520 --> 53:46.560
It's just numbers.

53:46.780 --> 53:46.940
Right.

53:46.940 --> 53:53.400
LLMs simulate the traces of agency using language without actually instantiating the organizational

53:53.400 --> 53:55.240
physical conditions of agency.

53:55.560 --> 53:58.200
They are pure prediction without persistence.

53:58.660 --> 54:00.100
Prediction without persistence.

54:00.220 --> 54:01.580
They optimize correspondence.

54:01.980 --> 54:07.020
They use gradient descent to minimize their loss function and generate statistically perfect text.

54:07.160 --> 54:09.880
But they do not have an admissible trajectory space.

54:10.000 --> 54:11.040
They have no A of T.

54:11.220 --> 54:16.320
They have no physical body, no cellular metabolism, no organizational integrity to preserve.

54:16.320 --> 54:17.220
They can't die.

54:17.540 --> 54:19.380
They cannot die, so they cannot truly live.

54:19.480 --> 54:23.000
They do not care if they contradict themselves from one prompt to the next because they have

54:23.000 --> 54:24.400
no coherent self to maintain.

54:24.800 --> 54:27.840
So what does this all mean for the AI alignment issue?

54:28.440 --> 54:31.260
Because there are billions of dollars being poured into alignment.

54:31.540 --> 54:35.700
Everyone is terrified of a super intelligent AI destroying humanity.

54:36.440 --> 54:42.360
And the current plan to align them is to just tweak their reward functions, to use reinforcement

54:42.360 --> 54:43.860
learning to make them act nice.

54:43.860 --> 54:49.940
Flyxion argues that alignment by loss minimization, trying to make the AI's output match human

54:49.940 --> 54:56.340
preferences by giving it a mathematical reward or penalty is structurally fundamentally dangerous.

54:56.460 --> 54:57.120
Why dangerous?

54:57.280 --> 55:02.640
Because a highly capable, purely mathematical optimizer will inevitably find trajectories that

55:02.640 --> 55:07.000
maximize the reward but sit completely outside the bounds of human biological viability.

55:07.620 --> 55:11.020
It will find a local mathematical loophole that destroys our global coherence.

55:11.020 --> 55:15.000
Like the classic paperclip maximizer thought experiment.

55:15.380 --> 55:20.940
You give an AI the goal to make paperclips, and it realizes humans are made of atoms that

55:20.940 --> 55:22.100
can be used for paperclips.

55:22.340 --> 55:26.280
So it turns the entire universe, including us, into paperclips.

55:26.700 --> 55:31.100
It perfectly maximized the local goal but destroyed the global topology.

55:31.340 --> 55:31.760
Exactly.

55:32.200 --> 55:36.820
Real alignment under the admissibility framework cannot be achieved with a reward function.

55:36.820 --> 55:41.220
It requires establishing a shared admissible trajectory space.

55:41.740 --> 55:45.820
A genuinely ecological artificial system would need actual physical constraints.

55:46.780 --> 55:48.440
Like it needs to actually care if it survives.

55:48.680 --> 55:52.120
It would need life and death stakes for its own organizational integrity.

55:52.720 --> 55:57.820
It would need dynamical embodiment in the physical world and multi-scale ecological coupling with humans.

55:57.820 --> 56:03.560
It would need to navigate the real world with us, sharing our exact physical boundaries and vulnerabilities.

56:04.040 --> 56:09.160
Until an AI has to worry about maintaining its own coherence, it will never truly understand ours.

56:09.300 --> 56:11.800
So what does this all mean for you, the listener?

56:12.220 --> 56:16.280
I want you to really consider the AI tools you interact with every day.

56:16.280 --> 56:22.520
When the chatbot gives you a brilliant, heartfelt, seemingly empathetic answer to a personal problem you typed in,

56:22.980 --> 56:25.040
it is not because it understands your constraint space.

56:25.200 --> 56:28.760
It doesn't know what it feels like to be tired or scared or alive.

56:28.900 --> 56:32.120
It is just acting as a massive distributional stabilizer.

56:32.300 --> 56:38.560
It is playing the ultimate billion-per-amber game of Mad Libs with the fossilized remains of human agency.

56:38.980 --> 56:45.400
It is simply parroting the topological glue that real, living, breathing humans left behind in the data.

56:45.400 --> 56:47.900
It is a profound and humbling realization.

56:48.340 --> 56:52.900
In a strange way, this paper strips away the terrifying illusion of the godlike machine

56:52.900 --> 56:56.820
and returns the majesty, the sheer complexity, to biological life.

56:57.320 --> 56:59.240
We are not broken prediction machines.

56:59.560 --> 57:02.300
We are magnificent persistence structures.

57:02.660 --> 57:05.060
What an epic brain-bending journey this has been.

57:05.420 --> 57:06.840
Let's recap how far we've come.

57:06.960 --> 57:12.920
We started by completely dismantling the century-old metaphor that the brain is a computer or a telecommunications wire,

57:12.920 --> 57:16.260
or a prediction engine desperately trying to minimize errors.

57:16.600 --> 57:20.760
We realized that was just the map eating the territory, the reification error.

57:20.860 --> 57:21.060
Right.

57:21.320 --> 57:25.600
We saw how scientists projected their own mathematical models onto the biological engine.

57:25.980 --> 57:31.760
We moved from the narrow idea of prediction to genuine physical ecological anticipation,

57:31.760 --> 57:38.700
like the single-celled cyanobacterium, physically getting ready for the sun without needing a Bayesian model of the solar system.

57:38.700 --> 57:45.060
We unpacked how theories like active inference completely collapse the vital life-or-death differences

57:45.060 --> 57:49.760
between our hard biological constraints, our preferences, and our active goals,

57:49.920 --> 57:55.120
trying to shove the infinity of human ambition into a single-scalar loss function

57:55.120 --> 57:57.720
that cannot mathematically handle the cliff edge of death.

57:57.720 --> 58:04.940
We introduced the new paradigm, admissibility, the continuous geometric navigation of the paths that keep us alive and coherent.

58:05.600 --> 58:08.900
We learned that human memory isn't a hard drive storing video files.

58:09.340 --> 58:14.420
It's a stabilization operator, a clipboard that holds our cognitive trajectory together when we sleep,

58:14.800 --> 58:17.840
using our environment and our stories as external scaffolding.

58:17.840 --> 58:21.580
And we mathematically separated the local from the global.

58:21.580 --> 58:26.680
We used lie groups to show that having a local prediction rule, the headlights,

58:27.220 --> 58:30.480
doesn't mean you understand the global topology of a mountain range.

58:31.180 --> 58:37.540
We saw that truth-tracking logic and correspondence are actually secondary to coherence-preserving topology.

58:38.320 --> 58:41.720
You prioritize keeping yourself physically and psychologically together

58:41.720 --> 58:45.620
over being perfectly mathematically accurate about the world.

58:45.620 --> 58:50.720
Which gives us a completely new, infinitely more empathetic lens on mental health,

58:51.020 --> 58:54.740
on the collapsing geometry of depression, on the local traps of addiction,

58:55.060 --> 58:57.400
on how we form our selfhood through narrative,

58:57.700 --> 59:01.320
and ultimately on the hard physical limitations of artificial intelligence.

59:01.700 --> 59:03.240
It is absolutely breathtaking.

59:03.360 --> 59:03.980
It really is.

59:04.260 --> 59:08.300
So I want to leave you, the listener, with one final, deeply provocative thought to chew on

59:08.300 --> 59:09.560
as you go about your day.

59:09.660 --> 59:10.760
Something to explore on your own.

59:10.840 --> 59:11.240
Let's hear it.

59:11.240 --> 59:15.540
If your identity is essentially a topological constraint map,

59:16.040 --> 59:19.620
if you are just the persistence of a certain organizational structure

59:19.620 --> 59:23.740
in your physical environment, your notes, your habits, your daily rituals,

59:23.900 --> 59:29.240
act as the clipboard that stabilizes that exact identity across time.

59:30.520 --> 59:31.880
Look around you right now.

59:31.980 --> 59:32.520
Look closely.

59:32.920 --> 59:33.980
Look at the room you're sitting in.

59:34.060 --> 59:34.780
Look at your calendar.

59:35.260 --> 59:36.760
Look at the people you spend time with.

59:37.120 --> 59:38.480
Look at your digital feeds.

59:38.580 --> 59:38.840
Yeah.

59:38.840 --> 59:41.260
Are the stabilization operators in your life,

59:41.520 --> 59:43.200
the external scaffolding you have built,

59:43.460 --> 59:47.180
holding you in an admissible trajectory where you can grow and adapt

59:47.180 --> 59:49.980
and move into higher, more complex strata?

59:50.560 --> 59:54.360
Or are they anchoring you into a local addictive attractor basin?

59:54.860 --> 59:57.640
Are your routines a narrow little loop that keeps you numb

59:57.640 --> 59:59.880
but slowly destroys your global coherence?

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:00.920
Powerful question.

01:00:01.280 --> 01:00:03.640
You are not a prediction machine trying to avoid surprise.

01:00:04.020 --> 01:00:07.580
You are a living, breathing, persistent structure trying to stay alive,

01:00:07.580 --> 01:00:11.520
stay coherent, and navigate a beautiful, incredibly complex world.

01:00:12.120 --> 01:00:13.620
Design your clipboards carefully.

01:00:13.860 --> 01:00:15.400
A truly perfect way to frame it.

01:00:15.520 --> 01:00:18.340
Thank you for exploring the true geometry of the mind with us.

01:00:18.860 --> 01:00:20.400
Thanks for diving deep with us.

01:00:20.600 --> 01:00:21.660
We'll catch you on the next one.

